WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.796
EF Core applications often use connection strings at design time, migration time, seeding time, and runtime.

2
00:00:06.796 --> 00:00:12.647
These contexts are related, but they do not always need the same identity or privilege level.

3
00:00:12.647 --> 00:00:18.688
Treating them as one connection string can make development easier but production review weaker.

4
00:00:18.688 --> 00:00:23.973
Design-time tooling should not require production credentials on developer machines.

5
00:00:23.973 --> 00:00:32.216
Developers need safe ways to scaffold, test, and run migrations locally without gaining unnecessary access to production databases.

6
00:00:32.216 --> 00:00:37.124
Local development should use local or development resources whenever possible.

7
00:00:37.124 --> 00:00:45.178
Migration credentials may need more privilege than runtime application credentials, especially when schema changes are involved.

8
00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:48.072
That privilege should be handled deliberately.

9
00:00:48.072 --> 00:00:56.064
A deployment pipeline might run migrations with a controlled identity, while the application runtime identity remains narrower.

10
00:00:56.064 --> 00:01:02.293
Automated deployment pipelines should protect migration connection strings and avoid printing them.

11
00:01:02.293 --> 00:01:10.976
Migration output, logs, command arguments, build summaries, environment dumps, and failure reports should be reviewed for secret exposure.

12
00:01:10.976 --> 00:01:15.444
Pipeline permissions should be limited to the environments they deploy.

13
00:01:15.444 --> 00:01:21.610
Runtime configuration should be explicit enough that the app connects to the intended environment.

14
00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:30.671
Test, staging, and production targets should be hard to confuse through naming, deployment approvals, platform configuration, and safety checks.

15
00:01:30.671 --> 00:01:35.075
A misdirected migration can be more damaging than a failed deployment.

16
00:01:35.075 --> 00:01:41.053
Seed data and database initialization should not embed secrets or sensitive connection details.

17
00:01:41.053 --> 00:01:50.932
Initialization logic should also be reviewed for environment assumptions, logging behavior, and whether it is appropriate to run automatically in production.
